This TypeScript function is supposed to sum every element of the array, but it always misses the last one. Fix it.

function sum(xs: number[]): number {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < xs.length - 1; i++) {
    total += xs[i];
  }
  return total;
}

This Python function is supposed to remove duplicates from a list while preserving the original order, but it sometimes skips elements. Find the bug, explain it, and give a corrected version.

def dedupe(items):
    seen = set()
    for i, x in enumerate(items):
        if x in seen:
            items.pop(i)
        else:
            seen.add(x)
    return items

Also make sure the caller's list is not mutated.

This Java class is stored in a HashSet, but contains() returns false for logically equal objects after one is mutated, and equals/hashCode are inconsistent. Identify every problem and rewrite the class correctly.

class Point {
    int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        Point p = (Point) o;
        return x == p.x;
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() {
        return x + y;
    }
}

Explain what contract is violated and whether you would make the class immutable.

Review this Go cache for concurrency bugs and fix them. Explain each race precisely, and make sure the loader runs at most once per key even under many concurrent callers (without holding a global lock during the load).

type Cache struct {
    mu    sync.RWMutex
    items map[string]string
}

func (c *Cache) Get(key string, load func(string) (string, error)) (string, error) {
    c.mu.RLock()
    v, ok := c.items[key]
    c.mu.RUnlock()
    if ok {
        return v, nil
    }
    v, err := load(key)
    if err != nil {
        return "", err
    }
    c.items[key] = v
    return v, nil
}

Provide the corrected implementation (standard library only, you may build a singleflight-like mechanism yourself) and a test that would trip the race detector on the original.

In PostgreSQL at READ COMMITTED, a hospital app enforces "at least one doctor must be on call per shift" with this logic in two concurrent transactions:

BEGIN;
SELECT count(*) FROM on_call WHERE shift_id = 7 AND doctor_id <> :me;   -- must be >= 1
DELETE FROM on_call WHERE shift_id = 7 AND doctor_id = :me;
COMMIT;

Two doctors run it at the same time and both succeed, leaving nobody on call. Explain exactly why, name the anomaly, and give at least three distinct fixes (e.g. SERIALIZABLE with retry, explicit locking, a constraint/materialized lock row). For each, discuss correctness, performance and failure handling, and write the code for the one you recommend.
